{"The reconstructed `RecoveryAuthnCodesCredentialModel` loses the stored credential's ID. `createFromValues` does not call `setId()`, so the returned model will have a `null` ID. Add `model.setId(myUser.recoveryCodes.getId())` after the `createFromValues` call to preserve the original credential ID.": 0.8926126956939697, "`getMyUser(user)` can return `null` for an unknown user, causing a `NullPointerException` at `myUser.recoveryCodes`. Add a null check like the other credential methods do (e.g., `isConfiguredFor`).": 0.8518965244293213, "`getCredential(...)` can return a user-storage credential, but the code below still removes and evaluates it as if it were a local stored credential. That breaks the \"last code used\" cleanup/prompt logic for federated users and can also inspect the wrong credential when both sources exist.": 0.8497622013092041, "Persisting recovery codes through the generic user-storage path can make them reusable for federated users, because user-storage validators are not required to consume a code after successful use.": 0.8781120777130127}